From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 22:25:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 183/185] Sega CD: the backup RAM file is EMUTOS, and AROS goes

We built an EmuTOS port. Naming the volume AROSDISK after the operating
system we did not build was funnier than it was useful, so it is EMUTOS
-- which is also what the console's own Backup RAM Manager will show
beside a game's saves, where a name that says what it is beats a joke.

Measured after the rename, unchanged in every respect: footer intact,
1 file, 5 blocks free, our FAT12 at byte 66, and no AROS string anywhere
in the image.

No fallback for the old name. A volume formatted in the few hours the
first spelling existed reads as unformatted, and on the internal 8 KB
there is no room to make a second file, so it needs the old save
deleting from the manager screen first. That is one sentence in
docs/bram-filesystem.md against a compatibility path carried forever.

Also gone, and this part is a deliberate divergence from upstream rather
than our own mess: the two "inspired by AROS" comments in bios/amiga.c
and the matching line in doc/authors.txt.

I argued against that and was wrong about the strength of it. There is
no AROS code in EmuTOS -- bios/aros.c, bios/aros2.S and
doc/license_aros.txt went years ago along with CONF_WITH_AROS, and what
remained was four comment lines crediting the AROS project for hardware
knowledge behind detect_gayle() and part of trackdisk, in Amiga-only
code this machine never executes. Not a licence obligation, no APL file
left to create one. So it was a choice, not a requirement, and the
owner's to make.

Desktop 26213da8979c5fbb on disc and cartridge.
---
 bios/amiga.c    | 6 +-----
 bios/segacd.c   | 8 ++++----
 doc/authors.txt | 1 -
 3 files changed, 5 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/amiga.c b/bios/amiga.c
index 9c66058..ee95c77 100644
--- a/bios/amiga.c
+++ b/bios/amiga.c
@@ -226,9 +226,7 @@ static BOOL is_custom_chips_mirror(void *mirror_base)
 
 int has_gayle;
 
-/* Detect A600 / A1200 Gayle chip.
- * Freely inspired by AROS ReadGayle().
- * https://repo.or.cz/AROS.git/blob/HEAD:/arch/m68k-amiga/exec/readgayle.S */
+/* Detect A600 / A1200 Gayle chip. */
 static void detect_gayle(void)
 {
     UBYTE gayle_id;
@@ -1518,8 +1516,6 @@ BOOL amiga_can_shutdown(void)
 /* Floppy                                                                     */
 /******************************************************************************/
 
-/* Some parts of this code have been inspired by AROS trackdisk.device.
- * https://repo.or.cz/AROS.git/blob/HEAD:/arch/m68k-amiga/devs/trackdisk/trackdisk_hw.c */
 
 static ULONG delay3ms;
 static ULONG delay15ms;
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index c818d3b..776df61 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -4712,7 +4712,7 @@ static ULONG segacd_buram_info(BOOL again)
  * The old arrangement wrote our FAT12 over the whole memory, footer and
  * all, so a cartridge could carry our files or a game's saves and not
  * both. This is the other way round: Sega's volume stays Sega's, and our
- * filesystem is one file in it called AROSDISK, sitting in the console's
+ * filesystem is one file in it called EMUTOS, sitting in the console's
  * own Backup RAM Manager beside whatever else is there.
  *
  * Sector i of the volume is bytes [i*512, i*512+512) of that file. The
@@ -4722,7 +4722,7 @@ static ULONG segacd_buram_info(BOOL again)
  * through that pointer, because a file's data takes a write in place and
  * the manager reads back what we put there.
  */
-#define BFS_NAME    "AROSDISK"          /* 11 chars, zero padded */
+#define BFS_NAME    "EMUTOS"            /* up to 11 chars, zero padded */
 
 static UBYTE *bfs_base;                 /* the file's data, or NULL */
 static UBYTE  bfs_tried;                /* asked the manager once already */
@@ -4772,7 +4772,7 @@ static BOOL bfs_init(void)
     return brmc->cs ? FALSE : TRUE;
 }
 
-/* Find AROSDISK. Sets bfs_base and bfs_sects, or clears them. */
+/* Find our file. Sets bfs_base and bfs_sects, or clears them. */
 static BOOL bfs_find(void)
 {
     bfs_base = NULL;
@@ -5269,7 +5269,7 @@ LONG segacd_bram_rw(WORD rw, LONG sector, WORD count, UBYTE *buf)
      * FORMATI.PRG formats by writing sectors through Rwabs, so this is
      * where "make me a filesystem here" actually arrives -- there is no
      * other entry point to hook. If the memory holds Sega's volume and
-     * has no AROSDISK in it yet, the file is created now and the sectors
+     * has no file of ours in it yet, one is created now and the sectors
      * land inside it, instead of going raw and taking the volume's
      * footer, and somebody's saves, with them.
      *
diff --git a/doc/authors.txt b/doc/authors.txt
index 3996187..eb12f79 100644
--- a/doc/authors.txt
+++ b/doc/authors.txt
@@ -22,7 +22,6 @@ Minor borrowed stuff:
 - the processor/FPU detection is taken from the MiNT kernel;
 - "Bug" includes parts of the original gettext source code;
 - some low-level hardware stuff comes from the Linux kernel;
-- parts of Amiga floppy driver and AUTOCONFIG are inspired from AROS;
 
 
